2. Specifics of the Infrastructure
For every additional element, why you are adding it:
3 Firewalls (1 per server): Added to secure each server at the network level by blocking unauthorized access ports and allowing only strictly required traffic.

1 SSL Certificate: Added to enable TLS encryption on the Load Balancer for www.foobar.com, securing user data during transit over public networks.

3 Monitoring Clients: Added to gather real-time performance data, resource utilization, and log files from each individual server for central analysis.

What are firewalls for:
Firewalls act as security barriers that filter incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined security rules. They prevent unauthorized port access, protect against port scanning, and mitigate malicious unauthorized connections.

Why is the traffic served over HTTPS:
Serving traffic over HTTPS ensures Confidentiality, Integrity, and Authenticity. It encrypts sensitive data (passwords, cookies, personal details) exchanged between the user's browser and the server, preventing Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks, eavesdropping, and data tampering.

What monitoring is used for:
Monitoring provides real-time visibility into infrastructure health. It allows administrators to track system metrics (CPU, RAM, Disk, Network), detect service outages instantly, analyze performance bottlenecks, and receive automated alerts before system issues cause user-facing downtime.

How the monitoring tool is collecting data:
Monitoring clients (agents) run as background daemon processes on each server. They collect system metrics directly from OS interfaces (e.g., /proc filesystem), tail server log files (e.g., /var/log/nginx/access.log), and periodically query local service status endpoints before securely pushing the telemetry data to a centralized monitoring server or cloud service (e.g., Sumo Logic).

Explain what to do if you want to monitor your web server QPS (Queries / Requests Per Second):
To monitor Nginx QPS:

Enable Nginx's status module (stub_status) in the web server configuration file.

Configure the local monitoring agent (or Prometheus exporter) to query the /nginx_status endpoint periodically to fetch the total request count (handled requests).

Calculate QPS by taking the rate of change of total request counters over time:
$\text{QPS} = \frac{\Delta \text{Requests}}{\Delta \text{Time (seconds)}}$.
3. Issues with this Infrastructure
SSL Termination at Load Balancer Level:

Because SSL/TLS is terminated at the Load Balancer, internal traffic transmitted between the Load Balancer and backend Web Servers (Server 2 and Server 3) travels over plain HTTP (unencrypted). If an attacker gains access to the internal network, they can sniff unencrypted backend traffic.

Only One MySQL Server Capable of Accepting Writes:

Having a single Primary (Master) MySQL database means write operations still have a Single Point of Failure (SPOF). If Server 2 fails, the application cannot process any write requests (such as user signups or post creations), resulting in partial system failure.

Servers with All the Same Components (Database, Web Server, App Server):

Resource Contention: Heavy database queries will consume CPU, RAM, and Disk I/O needed by Nginx and the Application Server, causing performance degradation across the entire node.

Blast Radius & Scalability Limitations: A failure in one component (e.g., a MySQL crash due to running out of RAM) brings down the Web and Application servers on that host as well. It also prevents scaling database hardware independently from web/app servers.